# The pieces a bare boolean cannot say. A test is a constant whose name begins
# `test_`, and `==` is the assertion — nothing here adds a second way to write
# a test that can already be written.
#
# A failure is a box (#2087), so these arms take the box and open it with a
# rescue. Every tested package's failures are foreign to this hako, which is
# what licenses the rescue: no builtin, no file gating, no exemption.
type caught

pub fn failed? e:<some>effect
  rescued? (e .? (_ -> caught))

pub fn failed? _
  false

fn rescued? caught
  true

fn rescued? _
  false

# Asserting WHICH failure. The failure is rescued here, where the raiser is
# foreign, and the caller receives its reason — a value a licensed party has
# already taken out of the box, so the tested package may read its own reason
# without rescuing its own failure.
#
# A non-failure answers false, so a test that expected a failure and got a
# value fails rather than passing quietly.
pub fn when_failed e:<some>effect k
  e .? (u -> k u.reason)

pub fn when_failed _ _
  false
